#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054470 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de7d614-76c9-5133-a4df-95f079467cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality With No Constant Term Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/id #uuid "b4b933f0-f414-5681-a466-0f070c903a41" :question/math-academy-id "e-5174" :question/problem "Find the solution of $x^2-3x \\lt  0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x & <0 \\\\ x(x-3) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x-3)=0.$ The roots are $x=0$ and $x=3,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty,0),$ $(0,3),$ and $(3,\\infty).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x(x-3)$ | $({\\color{red}-})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{blue}+}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{red}-}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{blue}+})={\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x-3) \\lt 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x-3)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(0, 3).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x^2-3x \\lt 0$ for all $x\\in (0,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054471 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/id #uuid "79c8bc06-0c46-51d6-89fe-daa3a6832247" :question/math-academy-id "e-5175" :question/problem "Find the solution of $x^2-3x +2\\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x+2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x-1)(x-2)=0.$ The roots are $x = 1$ and $x=2,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty,1),$ $(1,2),$ and $(2,\\infty).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $(1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x-1)(x-2)$ | $({\\color{red}-})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{blue}+}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{red}-}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{blue}+})={\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x-1)(x-2) \\geq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-1)(x-2)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty, 1)$ and $(2,\\infty).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $(x-1)(x-2) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty, 1] \\cup [2, \\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054472 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/id #uuid "e73ff639-68a7-57b0-93af-05567e2bc44f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5176" :question/problem "Find the solution of $x^2+x \\leq 6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x & \\le 6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+3) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x-2)(x+3) = 0.$ The roots are $x=2$ and $x=-3,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty, -3),$ $(-3,2),$ and $(2,\\infty).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $(-3,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x+3)(x-2)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x-2)(x+3) \\leq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-2)(x+3)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-3,2).$ Also, we know that the expression equals zero at the points $x=-3$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $(x-2)(x+3) \\leq 0$ for all $x\\in[-3,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054473 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b23c4da-7a8d-5076-a4e9-8bcde40bc088" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table of Signs"}] :question/id #uuid "fe73a302-d48f-504f-8b8d-553f4ecd9237" :question/math-academy-id "e-5191" :question/problem "Complete the following sign table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ |  |  |  |\n| $x+1$ |  |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We need to fill in each box with the sign of the corresponding expression on the corresponding interval.\n\nLet's start down the first column. In the interval $(-\\infty, -1),$ we choose a test point $x_1=-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-2-2=-4<0, \\\\ x+1 & =-2+1=-1<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso both the expressions $x-2$ and $x+1$ are negative $({\\color{red}-})$ on the interval $(-\\infty,-1).$\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ |  |  |  |\n\nTo find the sign of $(x-2)(x+1)$ on this interval, we can just multiply the corresponding signs of $x-2$ and $x+1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-}) \\times ({\\color{red}-}) = ({\\color{blue}+})\n$$\n\nSo $(x-2)(x+1)$ is positive $({\\color{blue}+})$ on this interval, and we can complete the first column of the table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |  |  |\n\nTo find the entries in the other columns, we use the same procedure.\n\n- In the interval $(-1,2),$ we can choose a test point $x_2=0.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0-2=(-), \\\\ x+1 & =0+1=(+), \\\\ (x-2)(x+1) & =(-)\\times (+)=(-),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |\n\n- In the interval $(2,\\infty),$ we can choose a test point $x_3=3.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =3-2=(+), \\\\ x+1 & =3+1=(+), \\\\ (x-2)(x+1) & =(+)\\times (+)=(+),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de7d614-76c9-5133-a4df-95f079467cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality With No Constant Term Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32ba100b-6df6-5023-8289-f06705215c46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-13,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277346} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb47c4d-01dd-5db6-a580-6907a474cd0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277347} {:answer/id #uuid "dbce4960-94c0-541d-ba2c-7ff1c35b85c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-13)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277348} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2c9d9b-b6b9-5652-bc8d-b7e032690797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-13)\\cup (13,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277349} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d7f28b-68f4-50ad-82e8-134b284d3037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (13,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32ba100b-6df6-5023-8289-f06705215c46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-13,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277346} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac145411-aa8b-5f50-ab05-857a3689f4d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec194c35-920f-572c-ad59-5ff817448032" :question/math-academy-id "q-115426" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+13x<0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+13x & <0 \\\\ x(x+13) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x+13)=0.$ The roots are $x=-13$ and $x=0,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-13),$ $(-13,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-13)$ | $(-13,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+13$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x+13)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x+13)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x+13)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-13,0).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+13x<0$ for all $x\\in (-13,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de7d614-76c9-5133-a4df-95f079467cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality With No Constant Term Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5cad427-888c-58ee-9017-88c094335c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-11)\\cup (11,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277353} {:answer/id #uuid "f41b1d81-eddb-585c-90fd-df8ebf3a2dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-11,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277354} {:answer/id #uuid "aa33b075-b3d2-5edc-ad6c-233f1e9f8b65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277355} {:answer/id #uuid "955ff952-435e-51dd-9bd9-da57dbd8f236" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (11,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277356} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f12b44-a47f-5482-b998-dfe0b0f81834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-11)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1f12b44-a47f-5482-b998-dfe0b0f81834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-11)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277357} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e654c33-8692-5b2d-8212-192f7f7fe456" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1184723f-f681-5872-85f4-eef5244ff4da" :question/math-academy-id "q-115864" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+11x>0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+11x & >0 \\\\ x(x+11) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x+11)=0.$ The roots are $x=-11$ and $x=0$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-11),$ $(-11,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-11)$ | $(-11,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+11$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x+11)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(-)\\cdot (+)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x+11)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x+11)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-11)$ and $(0,\\infty ).$ \nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+11x>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-11)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6b4d919-b241-5f8a-9797-945dc454ba63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2]\\cup [6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277360} {:answer/id #uuid "c604dbfa-c683-55c1-a7ff-655b8c4b6f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-6]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277361} {:answer/id #uuid "18564375-43fb-545b-a274-a7a657a94fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277362} {:answer/id #uuid "3ac5914a-e708-5e6e-aeeb-215520b38e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-6,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277363} {:answer/id #uuid "34936396-c19c-5c23-856b-7a00e9fbc8b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-2,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18564375-43fb-545b-a274-a7a657a94fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277362} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bda8711-d367-577e-9daf-4c15bdfba0a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1fb90a8-d4e7-5eb6-a80a-75abbeea0f22" :question/math-academy-id "q-115871" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-4x-12\\ge 0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x-12 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-6) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+2)(x-6)=0.$ The roots are $x=-2$ and $x=6,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(-2,6),$ and $(6,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+2)(x-6)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+2)(x-6)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+2)(x-6)$ is zero o positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-2$ and $x=6.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-4x-12\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [6,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22038a8a-298b-515f-8b1e-49b90684a78e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-6,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277367} {:answer/id #uuid "3329922d-336c-550a-beb1-27e413a5d446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277368} {:answer/id #uuid "023b32bd-cbb1-53f8-a17b-def09463c8da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [3,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277369} {:answer/id #uuid "bc0bfc36-864c-5f29-8977-b8d18f15ee7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-6]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277370} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2458cf-2762-5c75-82ce-0ca429de7148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22038a8a-298b-515f-8b1e-49b90684a78e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-6,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277367} :answer-field/id #uuid "9431392e-a363-5bed-ac50-bc6859518803" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a677599-d36a-59c6-a5e7-084db3500cb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-116190" :question/problem "Find the solution of $\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+2x\\le x+6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+2x & \\le x+6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+3x-18) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x+6)(x-3) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+6)(x-3) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+6)(x-3)=0.$ The roots are $x=-6$ and $x=3,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-6),$ $(-6,3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-6)$ | $(-6,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+6$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+6)(x-3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+6)(x-3)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+6)(x-3)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-6,3).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-6$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+2x\\le x+6$ for all $x\\in [-6,3].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241f308e-1ced-5b83-809d-11928171a142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-2,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277374} {:answer/id #uuid "6f73456c-3cdc-5ca4-9162-8ef0b55dbc88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277375} {:answer/id #uuid "72a5ec07-ebec-5bac-8850-09b9a3e7723c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277376} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcc91ec-f459-5776-afde-007250e449f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277377} {:answer/id #uuid "347ac722-421b-5e16-aa32-8a41aff04053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72a5ec07-ebec-5bac-8850-09b9a3e7723c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277376} :answer-field/id #uuid "73760a9c-44eb-5b66-970e-190233fc907c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eac244c7-6dc3-5f12-a5c2-03fecec15ec2" :question/math-academy-id "q-116200" :question/problem "Find the solution of $\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}x\\ge 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}x & \\ge 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}x-2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}({x}^{2}+3x-10) & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(x+5)(x-2) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+5)(x-2) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+5)(x-2).$ The roots are $x=-5$ and $x=2,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-5),$ $(-5,2),$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+5)(x-2)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+5)(x-2)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+5)(x-2)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-5$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}x\\ge 2$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b878c7b-3257-5de7-bc49-27a6b7619110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277381} {:answer/id #uuid "df034f3c-6078-56f9-9d37-bd922c822f55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277382} {:answer/id #uuid "d92621c9-2a41-5e9e-8dd0-14c9df127fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [3,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277383} {:answer/id #uuid "b04886da-d0b0-5584-9578-3aef3955e472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277384} {:answer/id #uuid "fba3302a-94a9-5c1d-b39b-9514a6c4e667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,-3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b878c7b-3257-5de7-bc49-27a6b7619110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277381} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c96296-4fbc-5ffa-af1f-a0c0f3bba073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8dc115d-b6e5-5817-ad88-e61421cd921e" :question/math-academy-id "q-27011" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-3x+7\\ge 5x-8?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x+7 & \\ge 5x-8 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+15 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x-3)(x-5) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x-3)(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=3$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,3),$ $(3,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x-3)(x-5)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x-3)(x-5)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-3)(x-5)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,3)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=3$ and $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-3x+7\\ge 5x-8$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "848342fc-9f75-506c-9e1f-84bc60fa69cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-2, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052302} {:answer/id #uuid "bee97ef2-4772-5ede-b714-55ad3fd3439d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-5, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052303} {:answer/id #uuid "f8cd13eb-3e34-5dde-a8c2-7ed0561e2380" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-2)∪(5, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052304} {:answer/id #uuid "2f849b33-75f3-5ec2-8c41-fe14d62df017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞, 2)∪(5, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052305} {:answer/id #uuid "36944fd7-c1f8-5a37-a131-1f943cf713b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-5)∪(2, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8cd13eb-3e34-5dde-a8c2-7ed0561e2380" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-2)∪(5, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052304} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0e024fc-3dc1-54a6-9f92-bc0201656bee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7824abae-ac0a-561f-a7e4-242996de64c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-27068" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-3x-10>0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x-10 & >0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-5) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+2)(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=-2$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(-2,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+2)(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+2)(x-5)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+2)(x-5)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (5,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-3x-10>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (5,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fdb5392-4c1b-5454-b0ac-de625db4f374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ [- 4, 3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052314} {:answer/id #uuid "d94eea31-3a28-5827-bd8f-5bc226e44fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ [3, 4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052315} {:answer/id #uuid "de5215b9-5c4f-552c-a2d2-872a4644c5a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ [- 3, 4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052316} {:answer/id #uuid "160e779f-ca0d-5036-ad8c-da02edee9899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: x ∈ (- ∞, 3]∪[4, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052317} {:answer/id #uuid "32345334-da2d-5928-8586-522d619a74dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/