$x-1$ | $-$ | {{field-2}} | $+$ |\n| $(x+5)(x-1)$ | $+$ | {{field-3}} | $+$ |" :question/worked-solution "On the interval $(-5,1),$ we can choose a test point ${x}_{1}=0.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =0+5=(+), \\\\ x-1 & =0-1=(-), \\\\ (x+5)(x-1) & =(+)\\times (-)=(-),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+5)(x-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b23c4da-7a8d-5076-a4e9-8bcde40bc088" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table of Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acd27b7d-da15-5ae6-923f-2dd85540c70f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277438} {:answer/id #uuid "cab97122-d66e-581f-9646-62ac8b63bc51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acd27b7d-da15-5ae6-923f-2dd85540c70f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277438} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd15b458-699c-5dfb-825f-42504c6e658d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277437} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c382f36a-ee00-54b4-917e-77ac75368fbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277441} {:answer/id #uuid "28de9203-0423-52d1-ab92-3f641013bf6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28de9203-0423-52d1-ab92-3f641013bf6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277442} :answer-field/id #uuid "761b8623-ec73-5df1-b5cf-14a18b9239d1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277440} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbc0cb61-bf68-55cd-ba21-892c66712745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277444} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e71161-1ee6-5bef-bc54-333fa08d77eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0e71161-1ee6-5bef-bc54-333fa08d77eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277445} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c00660d-21a6-5eec-a22a-5fafddcf0fc8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac117008-5390-5446-ad55-241a34126660" :question/math-academy-id "q-278489" :question/problem "What are the missing entries in the sign table below?\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,8)$ | $(8,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ |\n| $x-8$ | $-$ | {{field-2}} | $+$ |\n| $(x+5)(x-8)$ | $+$ | {{field-3}} | $+$ |" :question/worked-solution "On the interval $(-5,8),$ we can choose a test point ${x}_{1}=1.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =1+5=(+), \\\\ x-8 & =1-8=(-), \\\\ (x+5)(x-8) & =(+)\\times (-)=(-),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,8)$ | $(8,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-8$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+5)(x-8)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e46f2f2e-3718-5263-a8e4-eeea7a1c8a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277448} {:answer/id #uuid "3e674203-a65e-5cda-bfd9-9c7901c10af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e674203-a65e-5cda-bfd9-9c7901c10af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277449} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7a97e23-f35e-50df-8a0c-b5dff7854ded" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277447} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "020e304c-92be-5cd5-ac22-6c19b1f3a27d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "020e304c-92be-5cd5-ac22-6c19b1f3a27d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277451} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8aef263-134e-5711-815a-ca189943d7a4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f55940ec-31be-5597-9289-8ecebc2cb69f" :question/math-academy-id "q-278541" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+8x+8<x+2?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8x+8 & <x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+6 & <0 \\\\ (x+6)(x+1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+6)(x+1)=0.$ The roots are $x=-6$ and $x=-1,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-6),$ $(-6,-1),$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-6)$ | $(-6,-1)$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+6$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+6)(x+1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+6)(x+1)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+6)(x+1)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-6,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+8x+8<x+2$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-6,-1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f498aa5-610b-532b-b4d2-cad59c2b1690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f498aa5-610b-532b-b4d2-cad59c2b1690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277454} :answer-field/id #uuid "291b5803-443c-5a1e-b7f3-1950fca5da99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277453} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "637db1ce-f2b8-5947-9d53-ea95c72af0d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "637db1ce-f2b8-5947-9d53-ea95c72af0d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277456} :answer-field/id #uuid "df2024ec-266b-5fcf-8530-2ca324e3c007" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cb3acdaf-a4ba-5f1f-96af-a06630c1ae3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-278585" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is $\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-2x\\le 9-6x?$\n\n$x\\in [$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$]$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-2x & \\le 9-6x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+4x-9 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}({x}^{2}+16x-36) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}(x+18)(x-2) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+18)(x-2) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+18)(x-2)=0.$ The roots are $x=-18$ and $x=2,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-18),$ $(-18,2),$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-18)$ | $(-18,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+18$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+18)(x-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+18)(x-2)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+18)(x-2)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-18,2).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-18$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-2x\\le 9-6x$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in [-18,2].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de7d614-76c9-5133-a4df-95f079467cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality With No Constant Term Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b08474a3-4bba-58d8-bb25-5b1292700cc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277459} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f3bc96-6016-514a-a5a1-2e233ea530bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277460} {:answer/id #uuid "417fd51e-d6bb-52e7-ae38-8de69fa1e20d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277461} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1b8cee-5de6-579d-b93b-45bc15eb03bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277462} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa9c0ef-d23c-53d3-a74e-7ef4c34f4e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfa9c0ef-d23c-53d3-a74e-7ef4c34f4e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277463} :answer-field/id #uuid "31779b32-2d92-597a-ac6a-c9d5cac4133c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af09d3a8-1383-5f69-bd64-b908058734c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-70962" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is $2{x}^{2}-8x>0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-8x & >0 \\\\ 2x(x-4) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $2x(x-4)=0.$ The roots are $x=0$ and $x=4,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ $(0,4),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $2x(x-4)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x-4)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $2x(x-4)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,0)$ and $(4,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $2{x}^{2}-8x>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2b5dcad-ae21-56c2-8748-ba07c25de5fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277466} {:answer/id #uuid "688b2ad3-f122-53b1-bae4-2ac375a7dd79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277467} {:answer/id #uuid "a1528a70-188a-5477-95f2-c739d765e228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277468} {:answer/id #uuid "1fcdafec-347c-50a1-acfe-aa21e48f120e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277469} {:answer/id #uuid "462c1903-cfd7-5a98-a477-990c5f1160ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "688b2ad3-f122-53b1-bae4-2ac375a7dd79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277467} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf5483cd-fb9f-51dc-875b-596a1eb42667" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7087269d-6958-56ac-8fc8-3f3260e1ba40" :question/math-academy-id "q-86809" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+5x+6>0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+6 & >0 \\\\ (x+3)(x+2) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+3)(x+2)=0.$ The roots are $x=-3$ and $x=-2,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,-2),$ and $(-2,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,-2)$ | $(-2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+3)(x+2)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+3)(x+2)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+3)(x+2)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-2,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+5x+6>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-2,\\infty ).$"}]}
